<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jami' al-Tawarikh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jamiʿ al-Tawarikh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jami">Jami</a>' al-Tawarikh, or Compendium of Chronicles, is a universal history compiled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, in the early fourteenth century. It covers the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and many other peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a> and was richly illustrated. It reflects the scholarly ambitions of Ilkhanid governance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jami-al-tawarikh.jpg" alt="Jami' al-Tawarikh" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rashid al-Din · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mongol_soldiers_by_Rashid_al-Din_1305.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Jāmiʿ al-Tawārīkh was written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din Hamadani</a> (1247 to 1318) at the start of the 14th century and has been called the first world history. The Ilkhan Ghazan commissioned it because he wanted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, now settled and adopting Persian customs, to remember their nomadic roots. It first set out the history of the Mongols and their predecessors on the steppes, as the Taʾrīkh-ī Ghazānī. To compile it Rashid al-Din set up a precinct at the Rab'-e Rashidi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, with a mosque, hospital and library. After Ghazan died in 1304 his successor Öljaitü asked for a history of the whole known world, finished between 1306 and 1311.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din</a> was born into a Jewish family at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>, became a physician at court, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> around the age of thirty, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> from 1304 and was executed on 13 July 1318, convicted of poisoning Öljaitü.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The work has four main parts. The Taʾrīkh-ī Ghazānī gives the history, genealogies and legends of the Mongol and Turkic tribes and the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> to Ghazan's death. A second part carries the reign of Öljaitü to 1310, and a universal history following al-Tabari's order from Adam to the pre-Islamic kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, Muhammad and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>, the Islamic dynasties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, the Turks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the Indians. The remaining parts are a set of five genealogies and a geographical compendium, which has not survived.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din</a> used written and oral sources: Juvayni's history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a>, the chronicle of Martin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opava">Opava</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the Altan Debter for the Mongols and translated Chinese works on medicine and administration. Scholars regard the work as collective, and some of it, including the period after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>'s death, is borrowed directly from Juvayni.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din</a> ordered one Arabic and one Persian copy each year for distribution across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>. About twenty illustrated copies were made in his lifetime, but only parts survive. The illustrations belong to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">Persian miniature</a> tradition and show Chinese influence, in dark outlines and transparent washes, and Byzantine influence in the figures. Surviving portions include an Arabic manuscript split between the Edinburgh University Library and the Khalili Collection, and two Persian copies of 1314 and 1317 in the Topkapı Palace Library, which later belonged to the Timurid ruler Shahrukh. The Arabic copy was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> in the 15th century and later passed to the Mughal court, including the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>, before the British acquired it in the 19th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jami' al-tawarikh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rashid al-Din's <b>Jamiʿ al-Tawarikh</b>, produced at the Ilkhanid court, is often described as the first universal history of Eurasia, drawing on Mongol, Chinese, Persian and European informants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In Iran the Ilkhan Ghazan became a Muslim in 1295, and his minister Rashid al-Din compiled the <b>Compendium of Chronicles</b>, an attempt at a history of the whole known world that drew on Chinese, Persian, Arabic, Turkic, Indian, and European sources and informants.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
